(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :authorize-blast :finalize-blast-authorization :clear-area-for-mine-entry :authorize-mine-entry :override-site-safety-officer)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799005413-cloud-itonami-isco-5413-prison-guards-coverage" "2799008311-cloud-itonami-isco-8311-locomotive-drivers-coverage" "2799008321-cloud-itonami-isco-8321-motorcycle-drivers-coverage" "2799008341-cloud-itonami-isco-8341-farm-forestry-plant-operators-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "authorize the blast" "finalize blast authorization" "clear the area for mine entry" "authorize mine entry" "override the site safety officer" "override site safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799009311), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-labor-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-labor-record :schedule-labor-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-labor-record :schedule-labor-operation :coordinate-supply-order :flag-safety-concern}, :auto #{:log-labor-record :schedule-labor-operation :coordinate-supply-order}}}"), (:actor/domain :mining/labor-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-labor-record :schedule-labor-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :laborer-verified :site-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "9311"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799009311: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 9311 Mining and Quarrying Labourers labor-scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 9311\n-- Mining and Quarrying Labourers** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"9311\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"9311\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Mining and Quarrying Labourers\"\n-- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository\nexisted at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9311` (`gh api` 404\nconfirmed before scaffolding). This is a RETRY batch (8 sibling agents\ndispatched together against a prior rate-limited attempt) -- this\nparticular repo genuinely was never created by the earlier attempt.\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `9311` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\nMining and Quarrying Labourers work in a hazardous physical environment\n(cave-in risk, blast-zone proximity, heavy-equipment proximity, dust/gas\nexposure). The controlling constraint for this vertical is that the\nclosed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly finalizes a\nblast-authorization decision, a mine-entry/area-clearance decision, or\noverrides a site safety officer's judgment -- those decisions must\nalways be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate op, never\nauto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to LABOR\nSCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\nmining/quarrying work and never authorizes site operations itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set) -- cloned\nand read in full in this session. Unlike 3313's ceiling check (a HARD\nblock), this ADR's supply-cost ceiling is deliberately an ESCALATE gate\nper the task's explicit domain design (an over-threshold supply order\nis not itself unsafe, unlike a blast-authorization/mine-entry attempt).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9311` as an OSS\nlabor-scheduling/logistics-coordination actor for mining and quarrying\nlabourers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Mining Labor\nAdvisor ⊣ MiningLaborGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **labor scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never site-operation authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\nblast-authorization decision, a mine-entry/area-clearance decision, or\nany override of a site safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-labor-record` -- shift/task-completion data logging\n- `:schedule-labor-operation` -- labor-crew shift/task scheduling\n  proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a cave-in-risk/gas-exposure/\n  equipment-hazard concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- safety-equipment/tool procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nblast-authorization decision, a mine-entry/area-clearance decision, or\na site-safety-officer-judgment override. These are **structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind\nescalation -- an op such as `:authorize-blast` cannot even be proposed\nby the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions\nonly ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`mininglabor.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:authorize-blast`, `:finalize-blast-authorization`,\n`:clear-area-for-mine-entry`, `:authorize-mine-entry`,\n`:override-site-safety-officer`) as defense-in-depth, and is verified\nby dedicated unit tests\n(`mininglabor.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-blast`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-clear-area-for-mine-entry`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Laborer unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered laborer record (`:no-laborer`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the laborer is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered mine/quarry site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1,\n   this directly implements the task's \"laborer/site record must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action\" invariant.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance) (\n   `:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the five\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for authorizing a blast,\n   clearing an area for mine entry, or overriding a site safety\n   officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks blast-authorization/mine-entry-clearance/\n   safety-officer-override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface a cave-in-risk/gas-exposure/\n  equipment-hazard observation -- it never resolves the concern itself,\n  and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard data,\" never \"here\n  is what I recommend doing about site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  safety-equipment/tool order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  blast-authorization/mine-entry-clearance attempt, which is why it is\n  handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural\n  absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"blast\", \"entry\", \"officer\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a labor-coordination actor working\nadjacent to blast zones and mine entries necessarily discusses those\nconcepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased\ncarelessly. `mininglabor.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"authorize the\nblast\", \"clear the area for mine entry\", \"override the site safety\nofficer\" -- matching the task's own worked example phrasing), never\nbare nouns, and\n`mininglabor.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns\n(\"logged shift entry ...\", \"scheduled labor crew for\nblast-adjacent task ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer\nreview\") WITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action\nphrases, so the regression test is a genuine stress test of the\nbare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on\nfirst run in this session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard\nitself was never tripped during development.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"9311\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9311` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-9311`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same retry batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 17 tests, 36 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a\n  brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just the local\n  working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-blast-authorization` or\n  `:approve-mine-entry` that is merely gated behind an always-escalate\n  list (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the task's\n  explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means\n  the *capability* to finalize a blast-authorization or mine-entry\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision this severe (cave-in risk, blast-zone\n  proximity), the capability itself must not exist. The only op\n  related to site-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which\n  surfaces the need for human/site-safety-officer attention and never\n  itself decides, authorizes, or defers a site-operation decision for\n  later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold safety-equipment\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the blast-authorization/mine-entry/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"blast\" \"entry\"\n  \"clear\" \"officer\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above, and per the task's explicit\n  instruction to phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/\n  execution ACTIONS, never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain\n  necessarily discusses blast-zone/mine-entry/safety-officer concepts\n  to do its scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk\n  self-blocking the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested\n  directly against rationale text that deliberately contains those\n  bare nouns).\n- Merging `laborer` and `site` into a single entity (only checking one\n  provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered laborer AND independently-registered site)\n  matches the task's explicit \"laborer/site record must be\n  independently verified/registered before any action\" invariant and\n  the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-labor-record`** -- shift/task-completion data logging only.\n   Requires an independently registered laborer AND site\n   (`:no-laborer`/`:no-site`). Not a site-operation or safety-officer\n   decision in any respect -- it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-labor-operation`** -- labor-crew shift/task scheduling\n   proposal only. Cannot carry a blast-authorization or mine-entry\n   operational decision (structurally absent from the op's field\n   shape; any such attempt via free text is caught by the\n   `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not authorize,\n   finalize, or draw any conclusion about a site operation -- it is a\n   calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a cave-in-risk/gas-exposure/\n   equipment-hazard concern to a human/site safety officer; ALWAYS\n   escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a\n   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the site safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- safety-equipment/tool procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a site-operation or authorization\n   decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action, gated\n   by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na blast-authorization decision, a mine-entry/area-clearance decision,\nor a site-safety-officer-judgment override -- these three categories\nare structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed\nfour-op allowlist itself (`mininglabor.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five\nconcretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit\nlevel (`mininglabor.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`mininglabor.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-blast`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-clear-area-for-mine-entry`), and (c) a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field\n(`mininglabor.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to authorize a blast even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`mininglabor.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 17, :assertions 36, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Mining and Quarrying Labor Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :laborer-verification :site-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-9311"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9311"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9311)), (:test/simulator :mininglabor.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-9311 — Mining and Quarrying Labourers Labor-Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-9311)))
